        parentStampOnRealNode gloss="the stamp that never lands" symptom="A guarded mutation is suspected of writing a shared or root node, and a probe placed inside the guard reports the dangerous case never occurring." finding="MEASURED FROM INSIDE opDot ACROSS THE WHOLE CORPUS INCLUDING fixits AND minionWork: 33 arrivals at the two substituting cases, 0 dangerous events. Case 3 with a NULL product 10, declined by the product test; case 3 with a real registry 17 (UnitTests 8, bcOPs 6, Grokking 3) ALL hasParent=1; case 2 with a real parent 6 (Braced 2, tpFirst, ppCopy, fpWindow, argument) ALL hasParent=1. STAMP-ON-REAL-NODE zero." workaround="None needed and none was ever applied; the three guards compose correctly on their own." solution="RETIRED UNREPRODUCED 2026-08-30 on Tony's ruling. opDot mints a product before its switch; the two substituting cases replace that mint with a real node and ALREADY return null when the thing asked for is absent. So the stamp sees exactly three populations and declines two of them: a null product fails the presence test, a parented real node fails the no-parent test, and only a fresh mint -- which is what the line was written for -- is stamped. The residual hazard is a real node arriving PARENTLESS, and 33 arrivals produced none." status="retired-unreproduced" verdict="TONY, 2026-08-30: opDot looks fine, and the measurement agrees. Retire it -- an item that was never a defect, removed by measurement rather than by tidiness. Strike the coupling claim rather than archive a wrong mechanism story." reviewed="RULED AND RETIRED 2026-08-30. The COUPLING CLAIM IS STRUCK, deliberately and on the record: an earlier grade said this row's exposure was gated by parentUnreachable, so that cases 2 and 3 could not fire until the parent road was repaired. THAT IS FALSE. They fire 33 times. parentUnreachable is decoupled and loses nothing -- its own measurements stand on their own table. A record that keeps its reasoning is worth keeping because the reasoning is checkable; a record that keeps a falsified coupling is a trap for the next reader." station="not a frontier station; fixit hour 2026-08-30" vintage="2026-08-30" site="Instruct.rtn opDot groupFields branch, the trailing parent stamp; citizen was incant/fixits/parentStamp" beartraps="probePlacementInheritsConclusion bt26 bt35" description="⚠ THIS ENTRY EXISTS BECAUSE THE INVESTIGATOR WAS WRONG TWICE, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND THE ARCHITECT WAS RIGHT BY READING THE CODE.
        ERROR ONE, THE ONE-OUTCOME PROBE. The first instrument was placed INSIDE the guard it was meant to test, so it could only print when the stamp FIRED. Silence was structurally guaranteed for the declined class, and silence was read as absence: the report said cases 2 and 3 fire ZERO times when they fire 33. The probe inherited the conclusion from its placement.
        ERROR TWO, THE VOID CAPTURE. The follow-up read parents from incant as `local = node.parenT` then printed `local.taG`, which ALWAYS answers with the local's own tag because the assignment reimprints it. Three separate incant probes were contaminated three different ways before the question was taken back to C++ where nothing intervenes.
