        boundarySupplyNotSubstitutable gloss="the janitor had a second job" symptom="Removing a rule's tokenize term leaves child nodes hanging on unrelated registry entries, and the rule's own products stop evaluating as values." finding="tokenize did TWO jobs and only one was advertised. Its own comment says it gloms parent label components together into the label string -- that glom CONSUMED the labels that a rule's LABELED sub-terms produce. captureSpan inherited the capture and nobody inherited the collapse, so with the term gone the child labels persist on the product." workaround="A rule whose sub-terms are all noLabel is immune and migrates clean; a rule with labeled sub-terms is not. NumbeR is held at flag-ON with its term standing, its verified green state." solution="REMEDY 2026-08-23, and the fork was not needed: the attach was PREVENTED AT SOURCE by respelling NumbeR's sub-terms with the noLabel dash -- the shipped mechanism NamE had used all along. A sub-term that mints no label leaves nothing to hang. Five buy criteria all passed: load census 42 to 4, fleet 52 green rows byte-identical, loopBranchT 3/12/3 and the reproducer 15 then 17, dual-run MATCH 5 of 5, and the de-term gate green with spans byte-identical." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; SEQ 92 minted, SEQ 94 halved, SEQ 95 cleaved, SEQ 97 named" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 97" site="incant/grammar:35 numberSet= is labeled; :38 NamE's terms carry the noLabel dash; GroupActions.rtn:1568 tokenize's glom" beartraps="parentPopulationSplit" description="FOUR CUTS, EACH WITH ITS OUTCOME PRE-REGISTERED, ALL FOUR LANDING WHERE THEY WERE AIMED.
        C4 THE VENUE -- LOAD TIME, confined. audit() with no user parse: term-removed 42 loose of which 38 are numberSet children; term-standing control 4. Every firing-time story dies here.
        C1 THE ORDERING -- THE ACTION IS INNOCENT. A field-number construction traced at aCTionNumbeR's entry and exit:
            term standing   ENTRY children=0 first=(none) text=[406]   EXIT children=0
            term removed    ENTRY children=1 first=numberSet          EXIT children=1
        The child is already there BEFORE the action runs and the action does not touch it.
        C2 THE NamE CENSUS -- OUTCOME B, and it is the discriminator. NamE products read children=0 in the term-removed state, though NamE lost its own term a week earlier. NamE is not immune by luck: read the grammar.
            NamE     first-=[a-zA-Z] nameSet-^* tokenize     both sub-terms carry the noLabel dash
            NumbeR   numberSet=[0-9]+ FloaT? tokenize        numberSet is LABELED
        A noLabel sub-term produces no label, so there is nothing to hang. That is the whole of the asymmetry that has been unexplained since SEQ 92.
        C3 THE IDENTITY, direct-subscript road -- per-firing product nodes, all distinct:
            actionTypE child 0x104ea2000 · binTypE child 0x104ea5e80 · byReF child 0x104ea5d00
            the grammar's own numberSet term node 0x104500a00, a fourth pointer
        Neither loud miss fired: no aliasing, and no grammar structure leaking into products.
        ⚠ NOTHING ANOMALOUS WAS FOUND ANYWHERE. The mechanism is ordinary documented machinery plus one undocumented second job, and every step of it was named by elimination rather than by story. The five candidate mechanisms this defect attracted are all dead.";
        passthroughPercentDash gloss="a printf flag closes the passthrough" symptom="A newly added extern will not tok: the canary count of externs in the generated header drops to zero and the failure names the START of the enclosing function, which is fine, while the real offender is a character sequence inside a string literal." finding="A -% passthrough block is terminated by the literal two characters %- wherever they appear, INCLUDING inside a C string literal. A printf left-justify flag such as %-14s therefore closes the block early, and everything after it is emitted into the generated file as raw text." workaround="Never use a %- printf flag inside a passthrough. Plain %s and %p are safe, which is why every pre-existing passthrough in the tree is unaffected -- none of them left-justifies." solution="NOT GRADED. No ruling requested. It may be inherent to a scanner with no lexer, in which case there is nothing to fix and the workaround is the answer." status="open" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; met while building the parseSelfRecursion probe instrument, 2026-08-26" vintage="2026-08-26" site="Commands.rtn probeNode; the offending text was a fprintf format PN %(dash)14s" beartraps="bt24 bt29" description="THE DETECTOR IS THE STANDING ONE AND IT WORKED PERFECTLY: count the externs in the generated header after every retok. It went 318 to 0 on the first attempt and named the problem immediately as being of the wipes-the-extern-block family.
        ⚠ THE MISDIRECTION COST ONE WRONG FIX FIRST. The tok diagnostic read FAIL Body3 at the function opening line, so the first repair was to add a leading declaration to match canonOf's shape -- a reasonable read of the message, and wrong, because per bt29 the Body3 line names the start of the ENCLOSING function and never the offender. The second look found the %- inside the format string.
        SECOND OCCURRENCE THE SAME HOUR, DIFFERENT SHAPE: file-scope C++ cannot be written in a .rtn at all, because tok honours a passthrough only inside a function body. A file-scope -% block is emitted verbatim into the .mm and the compile fails on the stray characters. The standing workaround is the one gNewParseInFlight already uses -- put the global in a hand-written header.";
        connectiveDiscriminant gloss="alternation emitted as conjunction" symptom="A generated parse condition joins a rule's options with AND where any one of them should do, and a census of the emitted bodies finds not a single OR anywhere in the population." finding="setParse hangs builtinParsE and builtinActoR -- both noPrint decoration -- on every rule it touches, so hasAttributeS, which answers is-this-node-marked-up, reads TRUE for every walked rule and the OR branch is unreachable. MEASURED 2026-08-26 as a 2x2 over parser(Start), 55 bodies: with setParse running 36 AND / 0 OR, with setParse off 28 AND / 8 OR, gate spelling irrelevant in both columns. A SECOND MECHANISM SAT UNDER IT: setParse adds each decoration attribute BEFORE marking it noPrint, so addAttribute -- the flag writer -- cannot see that decoration is decoration at the one instant it looks, and a replacement flag asking the right question still answered wrongly. Both are fixed; the live row now reads 28 AND / 8 OR in both columns." workaround="None. Nothing is built on the connective being right yet, because no generated parse has been trusted for a whole-grammar answer." solution="FIXED IN TWO RULED PARTS. 2026-08-26: hasTraits, a GroupBody flag raised only by an attribute that is NOT noPrint-class, at four registration sites with addAttribute and updateContentFlags as writers, and generateParse's gate swapped to it. 2026-08-27: setParse calls field.updateContentFlags() once after both decoration attributes are fully formed -- a rebuild at exit rather than a reorder. A third edit was forced and unplanned: updateContentFlags read listLength through an unguarded groupList, latent while moveTo was its only caller, fatal the moment setParse handed it leaves. STEP-AND-BLESSED by Tony 2026-08-27; the citizen promoted out of the queue into the fleet as incant/connectiveT." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; parser iteration 2 docket, promoted by ruling 2026-08-26, discharged by remedy 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="seven sites: GroupBody.twk and groups.ext for the bit, incant/setup GroupFields, Instruct.rtn opDot case 42, GroupItem.twk addAttribute and updateContentFlags, Generate.rtn setParse, and IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser generateParse for the gate; fleet fixture incant/connectiveT, promoted 2026-08-27 from incant/fixits/connectiveDiscriminant, which is its VINTAGE name -- it promoted to the fleet as incant/connectiveT on 2026-08-27" beartraps="bt26 bt28 firstCallerNullList parseSelfRecursion" description="⚠ IT IS A CORRECTNESS THREAT AND NOT A CRASH CANDIDATE, and the distinction is the reason it got its own row rather than a line on the recursion docket. An alternation emitted as a conjunction produces a WRONG parse, never an unbounded one. So it survives every instrument the recursion work is using: a future parser(Start) that runs to completion could still parse incorrectly, with the verifier's printed ANSWER the only guard between a wrong parse and a green run.
        THE CHARTER, Tony's words: is the connective discriminant sound across all 123 rules the walk reaches. Queued BEHIND the recursion work.
        ⚠ THE BARE-NAME CANDIDATE IS DEAD, killed 2026-08-26 by the reading it asked for. Read against an explicit argument.hasAttributeS on the same node the two spellings agree, so generateParse was answering about the right node all along. What it was ASKING was the wrong question, which is the other half the citizen's own kill condition named.
        THE FIRST REMEDY PASS LANDED AND DID NOT DISCHARGE, and the 2x2 named the sole cause rather than eliminating one suspect: the gate spelling changed nothing in either column, setParse changed everything. The surviving defect was an ORDERING. setParse does addString first and sets noPrint second, and addString reaches addAttribute, which is where hasTraits is written -- so at the only instant the write gets to look, the decoration is not yet marked as decoration. A flag computed at add time cannot see a property the caller stamps after the add returns.
        THE SECOND PASS FIXED IT AT EXIT, one line, and the 2x2's live row moved 36 AND / 0 OR to 28 AND / 8 OR with setParse running, matching its OFF twin. StatemenT emits if SemI() OR BlocK() OR WardeD() OR Iterate() OR Xpress().
        ⚠ THE RETIRED GATE'S ROW DELIBERATELY DID NOT MOVE, and that is the negative control rather than a miss. hasAttributeS still reads 36 / 0 with setParse running, because the node IS marked up and that flag answers exactly that. Old gate still wrong, new gate right, same build: without the non-moving row the census could not attribute the change to the gate. It was pre-registered as a hold before the run, and it must never be "fixed".
        THE CHARTER QUESTION IS STILL OPEN on its own terms: soundness across all 123 rules is not shown by eight ORs appearing, only that the branch is reachable. It is settled by a parse that runs and answers, which waits on parseSelfRecursion.";
        firstCallerNullList gloss="dead helper meets its first caller" symptom="A long-dormant helper is given its first live caller and the process dies at EXC_BAD_ACCESS on a low address inside it, taking unrelated fleet rows down with it, while the edit that added the caller looks entirely innocent." finding="GroupItem::updateContentFlags read `if listLength` through an unguarded groupList -- groupBody->groupList->listLength, GroupItem.mm:2422 -- and had done so for as long as it existed. A LEAF has no list at all, so the read lands at address 0x10. It was latent because moveTo was the only caller in the tree; the moment setParse became the second one and began handing it every term it touches, leaves included, it died on the first listless one." workaround="None needed; the guard is the fix. Before it landed the containment was to not call the helper." solution="One guard, matching the idiom every other reader of that field already uses -- opDot case 5 and cases 403/404 both test groupList before dereferencing it. `if groupList && listLength`." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; met landing connectiveDiscriminant part two, 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="GroupItem.twk updateContentFlags; the caller that exposed it is Generate.rtn setParse" beartraps="bt19 connectiveDiscriminant" description="⚠ THE RULE IS THE INVERSE OF THE DEMOLITION-ARC ONE, and it is why this got a record rather than a line in a commit. That rule says READ CONDEMNED CODE BEFORE DELETING IT, because it is often the only written specification of what you are about to rebuild. This is the same asymmetry from the other end: A HELPER WITH NO LIVE CALLER HAS NEVER HAD TO SURVIVE ANYTHING. Its correctness has been asserted by nothing, so reviving it is not a free one-line change -- it is shipping unexercised code into a hot path, and every input class its old caller happened never to produce arrives at once.
        THE MISDIRECTION WAS TOTAL AND IS WORTH RECOGNISING. The ruled edit was one line in setParse. The crash was in a different file, in a function the ruling had described as ALREADY CORRECT -- correctly, as far as the flag logic went. The fleet named two parseClass rows that have nothing to do with connectives. Nothing pointed at a null list.
        WHAT MADE IT CHEAP: one backtrace, taken before any theorising, which named the line and the address. Bear-trap 19s corollary in its cheap direction -- the grep, or here the bt, beats the reasoning.
        ⚠ AND THE COST WAS PAID IN THE RIGHT ORDER ONLY BECAUSE THE BEFORE-CAPTURES EXISTED. The fleet went 57 to 55 and the two red rows were identifiable as caused rather than pre-existing purely by diffing against a capture banked before the first edit. Without it the parseClass rows would have been a mystery of unknown vintage.";
        parseSelfRecursion gloss="generated parse re-enters itself" symptom="A generated-parse walk completes and prints its sentinel, and then the verification call dies at SIGSEGV with tens of thousands of identical stack frames naming one action." finding="CONFIRMED BY PRINT 2026-08-27, in pointers. aCTionStatemenT dispatches statement->groupBody->gMethod(statement) at GroupRules.mm:1487; under a walked-and-compiled grammar that gMethod resolves to aCTionStatemenT itself, and the node handed down is THE SAME NODE every time -- input == statement true, and input identical at 0x...5f140 across 35 consecutive frames read at depths 6, 8, 20 and 30, tag StatemenT throughout. Period one, in pointers rather than by frame-shape inference. ⚠ AND getGroup IS NOT INVOLVED AT ALL: StatemenT reads datA 0, not isGROUP, so the guard above it is false and the call never fires. statement is the plain assignment from input and nothing between touches it. 74705 frames, guard page touched by the allocation at GroupRules.mm:1475." workaround="Turn either gate off. The crash needs BOTH compiling and activating on; any other cell of the 2x2 completes at exit 0 and reaches the verify sentinel." solution="CANDIDATE 2 PROMOTED TO RULED, 2026-08-27, on the confirming print. The mechanism is settled: one node, its own action, dispatched at GroupRules.mm:1487. ⚠ THE FIX SHAPE IS NOT SELECTED -- the ruling is about the DIAGNOSIS, and the fix conversation opens on that dispatch line. Nothing is buildable from this row yet." status="ruled" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; parser iteration 2, 2026-08-26; confirming print 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="GroupRules.mm:1487 is the recursing dispatch; GroupRules.mm:1475 is the allocation that touches the guard page; IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser is the caller, with BOTH gates on. ⚠ SECOND DRIVER, 2026-09-09: incant/trigDO39 -- SPLIT OUT OF incant/trigDO ON 2026-09-09 when trigDO became the ruling-(c') parse certificate and joined pop.sh; trigDO39 keeps the all-39 compile AND the frozen 2026-09-08 emitter, deliberately, because the recursion must be reproduced against the body that produced it -- reproduces it, and it is the cheaper driver because it is one tracked file that needs no offline working set. Measured by lldb with a breakpoint on aCTionStatemenT ignored 400 times -- every frame reads GroupRules.mm:1166, the input pointer is identical in every frame, and at the break the node reads tag StatemenT, isRule 1, gMethod 0x10001d2b4 which nm resolves to _aCTionStatemenT. ⚠ THE DISPATCH LINE HAS MOVED: :1487 at the 2026-08-27 vintage and :1490 at the 2026-08-29 one are the SAME CALL now standing at :1166, moved by intervening edits and not a second site. The entry question posed by the 2026-09-09 dispatch is ANSWERED YES -- it enters through the isMethod arm, the statement->groupBody->gMethod(statement) call with no legality check. ⚠ ERRATUM: the 2026-08-26 docket cited :1483 for getGroup. At that same vintage getGroup is at :1484 and :1483 is the isGROUP guard above it -- bear-trap 36 exactly, a line number naming the test rather than the call. The call is moot in any case; it never fires." beartraps="bt34 bt26" description="⚠⚠ THE DOOR, 2026-08-27 SECOND PRINT -- AND THE ENTRY PATH IS NOT WHAT THE runAction RECONSTRUCTION SAID. Read outermost-inward from the stack bottom, the frames below the first mirror entry are: runOP, runAction argument=StatemenT field=parser, processAction, aCTionBlocK, runOP, runOP, runRule field=ruleText rule=StatemenT, parseRule field=StatemenT, aCTionBlocK, then aCTionStatemenT. THREE CORRECTIONS. One, the runOP/runAction/processAction frames are the VERIFIER ACTION parser executing its own ordinary body, not a generated parse body -- runAction is running the action named parser with argument StatemenT, and the aCTionBlocK under it is parser's own block. Two, the rule-name call DID take the legal road: runRule then p